WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.940
Elevator Action, the ups and downs of the Spy Game.

00:00:19.080 --> 00:00:25.980
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:26.220 --> 00:00:27.860
I'm Mike. I'm Sean.

00:00:28.140 --> 00:00:30.720
And I'm Joe. No surprises there, guys.

00:00:30.940 --> 00:00:32.360
You all said your names correctly.

00:00:32.940 --> 00:00:35.080
And it's time to play some...

00:00:35.080 --> 00:00:36.200
Let me ask you real fast, Sean.

00:00:36.460 --> 00:00:38.080
You a guy who enjoys riding the elevators?

00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:40.640
I wear an elevator sometimes.

00:00:41.360 --> 00:00:42.720
Okay, I don't know what that means.

00:00:42.840 --> 00:00:44.860
I'm kind of concerned. Joe, you like elevators?

00:00:44.900 --> 00:00:47.840
I love elevators. I love that elevator action.

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:49.760
Oh, you know the game.

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:53.360
It is elevator action, as we alluded to just moments ago.

00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:56.420
This is another Taito game.

00:00:56.420 --> 00:00:57.900
No surprises there.

00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:03.300
They love to make arcade games that don't translate that well to console experiences.

00:01:03.740 --> 00:01:06.420
And it's Micronix again.

00:01:07.480 --> 00:01:08.720
Micronix is doing it.

00:01:09.040 --> 00:01:09.960
Taito's doing it.

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:13.340
And so the arcade game came out in 1983.

00:01:14.460 --> 00:01:17.260
And now it's on the NES in 1987.

00:01:18.520 --> 00:01:19.740
And boy, does it show.

00:01:20.300 --> 00:01:22.720
Yeah. And I just mean...

00:01:22.720 --> 00:01:23.960
So I don't want to, you know...

00:01:23.960 --> 00:01:27.020
We should talk about the plot and the objective before we go any further.

00:01:27.240 --> 00:01:33.260
But what I mean is, is just like, this game looks Atari 2600 level graphics, right?

00:01:33.360 --> 00:01:39.440
Am I wrong? Yeah, I can't think of many games that look as archaic as this one.

00:01:39.580 --> 00:01:41.020
Popeye was the last one for me.

00:01:41.120 --> 00:01:41.960
Yes, that's what I have in my head.

00:01:41.980 --> 00:01:44.100
I almost think Popeye looked better than this.

00:01:44.360 --> 00:01:46.720
Well, that's what I guess I... Yeah, but remember Popeye's boat?

00:01:46.760 --> 00:01:49.680
And then you see the boat in the arcade and you're like, wow, that's a boat.

00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:53.260
Right, right. I was playing on a bunch of zigzags, you know?

00:01:53.680 --> 00:01:55.240
That's how I feel with elevator action.

00:01:55.240 --> 00:01:59.380
Only the elevator action arcade game also looked pretty similar to that.

00:01:59.560 --> 00:02:04.440
Yeah. So here we go with the plot for elevator action, because I know everybody was looking for that

00:02:04.440 --> 00:02:10.260
deep lore. The player assumes the role of Agent 17, code name Otto.

00:02:10.700 --> 00:02:11.700
Now, here's...

00:02:11.700 --> 00:02:12.600
I gotta stop right there.

00:02:13.500 --> 00:02:15.360
I have to stop one sentence in.

00:02:15.520 --> 00:02:17.300
Isn't it usually the other way around?

00:02:17.580 --> 00:02:20.760
Yeah, I was just gonna say... Aren't you like Otto, code name Agent 17?

00:02:20.900 --> 00:02:24.960
No, you are... This is like a Hitman situation where you are the barcode, but you take on a name.

00:02:24.960 --> 00:02:25.980
Oh, okay.

00:02:26.220 --> 00:02:28.620
Interesting. I like that. I didn't know that was Hitman's thing, actually.

00:02:28.820 --> 00:02:33.460
Well, I don't actually know either, but I think it is Agent 17 in Hitman.

00:02:33.740 --> 00:02:35.440
Oh, I thought... Oh, that's right!

00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:36.640
Yeah. Oh, yeah!

00:02:36.940 --> 00:02:38.000
Is this a prequel?

00:02:38.500 --> 00:02:39.800
That is so strange.

00:02:40.120 --> 00:02:41.220
Anyway... Everything's a prequel.

00:02:42.260 --> 00:02:43.220
Everything. For us.

00:02:43.240 --> 00:02:44.300
No matter how... And who knows?

00:02:44.360 --> 00:02:46.180
Maybe Agent 17, right?

00:02:46.340 --> 00:02:48.420
This Otto, code name guy from Elevator Action.

00:02:48.780 --> 00:02:52.090
Maybe it's like his descendant is Hitman.

00:02:53.000 --> 00:02:54.360
That could be. That makes it a prequel.

00:02:55.460 --> 00:02:56.540
No, no, no. I meant like...

00:02:56.540 --> 00:02:57.520
It could be just... It could be young.

00:02:57.520 --> 00:03:00.420
I meant in a reference to the Athena episode last week, Sean.

00:03:00.560 --> 00:03:04.380
Oh, yeah, yeah, yeah. Exactly. In the Athena episode last week, we found out that Athena Asamiya is Athena's...

00:03:05.500 --> 00:03:06.840
Wow, how many times have I said that?

00:03:07.260 --> 00:03:09.820
I don't know. People are like, no less, two weeks.

00:03:09.820 --> 00:03:15.820
Okay. Anyway, Agent 17, codename Otto, he's a secret agent for an unspecified organization...

00:03:16.340 --> 00:03:17.360
Or government. In your country.

00:03:18.660 --> 00:03:20.060
Like, it doesn't specify.

00:03:20.060 --> 00:03:26.020
It doesn't even know. Organization or government who must collect all the documents and reach the basement using

00:03:26.020 --> 00:03:27.000
the elevator and stairs.

00:03:28.060 --> 00:03:34.080
So then, if we're gonna get into the gameplay, the player assumes the role of a spy infiltrating

00:03:34.080 --> 00:03:36.200
a 30-story building filled with elevators.

00:03:36.380 --> 00:03:40.560
The player is pursued by enemy agents who appear from behind closed doors.

00:03:40.860 --> 00:03:43.840
Your mission? Collect documents and get down to the basement.

00:03:44.960 --> 00:03:46.060
Where there is a car.

00:03:46.240 --> 00:03:49.820
Yeah, where the plot and the gameplay come together so seamlessly.

00:03:49.820 --> 00:03:50.960
I think there's something to be said.

00:03:51.080 --> 00:03:55.540
Yeah, there's something to be said, like, if you can have both be the same thing.

00:03:56.680 --> 00:03:58.600
Yeah, it was pretty much the same paragraph.

00:03:58.820 --> 00:04:03.040
It's kind of beautiful. So what do we think about the gameplay?

00:04:03.580 --> 00:04:08.800
I mean, I was surprised that there was so much shooting guns in this game for an NES game.

00:04:08.940 --> 00:04:10.400
I feel like they steered clear of that.

00:04:10.400 --> 00:04:12.260
Yeah, shooting the guns.

00:04:13.420 --> 00:04:15.720
We've had some very violent games so far.

00:04:15.720 --> 00:04:16.800
I guess that's true.

00:04:16.980 --> 00:04:18.680
But, like, we had, like, Commando and stuff.

00:04:19.060 --> 00:04:23.220
You remember the games where you took an actual gun and shot people in the face, Joe?

00:04:24.100 --> 00:04:26.180
What? Yeah, the Zapper games.

00:04:26.340 --> 00:04:27.200
You had an actual gun.

00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:29.020
That wasn't people? Oh, yeah, okay.

00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:29.860
I'm thinking of Duck Hunt.

00:04:29.940 --> 00:04:31.320
Hogan's Alley and stuff like that.

00:04:31.460 --> 00:04:32.780
Oh, yeah. Ducks aren't people.

00:04:33.140 --> 00:04:34.780
Well, that's true. But fair.

00:04:34.880 --> 00:04:36.120
There have been other gun games.

00:04:36.200 --> 00:04:38.620
But for some reason, this one just feels more...

00:04:38.620 --> 00:04:43.140
Maybe it's just, like, the bullets and, like, you're just killing people that are just,

00:04:43.140 --> 00:04:44.620
like, in an office building.

00:04:44.840 --> 00:04:48.480
All right, so, all right. So, all right. For some reason, Joe is very offended by the violence in this game.

00:04:48.480 --> 00:04:49.340
I don't know why.

00:04:49.340 --> 00:04:50.300
I'm not offended at all.

00:04:50.580 --> 00:04:54.400
I just meant that I'm surprised that they, you know, I feel like people were a lot more

00:04:54.400 --> 00:04:59.780
sensitive to that. But I think a good way to work around that is that I would have really enjoyed if this

00:04:59.780 --> 00:05:01.140
was a Zapper game, actually.

00:05:01.640 --> 00:05:05.680
Like, imagine if Agent 17 acts on his own, right?

00:05:05.880 --> 00:05:10.660
Agent 17 kind of, like, does his thing walking around trying to get to the bottom without avoiding

00:05:10.660 --> 00:05:17.040
contact. And then you are, like, the marksman across the other building shooting with the Zapper

00:05:17.040 --> 00:05:21.060
all of the different targets that show up trying to kill him as he completes the mission.

00:05:21.060 --> 00:05:23.340
Oh, wait, no, this has to be a multiplayer game, then.

00:05:23.480 --> 00:05:24.920
It can only be played co-op.

00:05:25.120 --> 00:05:28.960
No, I am saying that Agent, I'm saying Agent 17 maybe is controlled by Rob.

00:05:29.760 --> 00:05:35.260
He is moving around little gyro discs that put the, you know, that move, like, okay,

00:05:35.600 --> 00:05:37.100
Otto will now walk to the left.

00:05:37.220 --> 00:05:41.860
Wow. And all those weeks ago, we were talking about how they could not think of another Rob game,

00:05:41.940 --> 00:05:43.380
but we already made it for them.

00:05:43.400 --> 00:05:47.020
Not only did I make a Rob game, I made a game where the Rob and the Zapper come together.

00:05:48.060 --> 00:05:53.100
Okay? Since we're talking about gameplay, I am very impressed that you're able to control

00:05:53.100 --> 00:05:54.220
the elevators in this game.

00:05:56.100 --> 00:05:57.340
That's a reason for the rest?

00:05:57.640 --> 00:06:02.400
I suppose. I mean, I think at first it, like, takes a second for you to realize that you're controlling,

00:06:02.460 --> 00:06:04.140
because the elevators will move if you don't move them.

00:06:04.140 --> 00:06:07.660
That's what it was. I thought the elevators, because they move on their own, I thought that I was just, like,

00:06:07.660 --> 00:06:11.360
at their mercy, and then I just, like, I was like, oh, I got to duck, and instead

00:06:11.360 --> 00:06:12.660
of ducking, I went down.

00:06:12.960 --> 00:06:15.180
Yeah. I was like, huh, I'm God.

00:06:15.180 --> 00:06:22.300
It was a neat experience, but yeah, I mean, just this, like, you must have a very low bar

00:06:22.300 --> 00:06:26.100
for excitement if, like, I was very surprised that we could control the elevators.

00:06:26.480 --> 00:06:28.620
I just didn't know what was possible in this game.

00:06:28.680 --> 00:06:32.460
And if you thought I was surprised when I found out you can control the elevators, wait

00:06:32.460 --> 00:06:34.260
until I found out you can shoot the lights out.

00:06:34.480 --> 00:06:38.520
Yeah. Yeah, that was like, I found that out at the very end of my time playing, and I was

00:06:38.520 --> 00:06:39.960
like, what? I could have done this the whole time?

00:06:40.360 --> 00:06:43.740
It seemed to maybe get you more points, but didn't really do much else for you.

00:06:43.740 --> 00:06:46.580
I don't think they react as quickly to you if you have the lights out.

00:06:46.720 --> 00:06:48.060
And you definitely get more points.

00:06:48.160 --> 00:06:51.240
I think it's worth, like, 500 points instead of, like, 100.

00:06:51.500 --> 00:06:54.880
But you shoot any light in the building, and all lights in the building go out, like

00:06:54.880 --> 00:06:56.440
the way lights work. Yeah, exactly.

00:06:57.440 --> 00:06:58.780
You've broken the circuit.

00:06:58.780 --> 00:07:03.140
Everybody just has that one master light switch in their living room that turns off and

00:07:03.140 --> 00:07:04.560
on all the lights in the house.

00:07:05.620 --> 00:07:08.180
Is this the first stealth game?

00:07:09.260 --> 00:07:11.760
Huh. Would you say stealth, though?

00:07:12.140 --> 00:07:14.220
I mean, you're right out in the open.

00:07:14.460 --> 00:07:18.480
I mean, yeah, I was gonna say, like, is Super Mario Brothers a stealth game if I, like,

00:07:18.560 --> 00:07:21.660
don't attack anybody and I just jump around everything?

00:07:21.980 --> 00:07:28.060
Well, I think one of the biggest parts of stealth games is shooting out lights.

00:07:28.600 --> 00:07:29.640
Yes, it's true.

00:07:29.820 --> 00:07:31.880
I'd say it's... That was a big component of Splinter Cell.

00:07:32.020 --> 00:07:33.440
It checks that box.

00:07:34.080 --> 00:07:38.840
And you're also a spy, but that's, like, one of those, like, additional boxes.

00:07:39.080 --> 00:07:40.160
Like, those are bonus boxes.

00:07:40.280 --> 00:07:41.120
Bonus boxes.

00:07:41.300 --> 00:07:42.780
Um, and...

00:07:42.780 --> 00:07:44.700
How's this on the spy scale right now?

00:07:44.760 --> 00:07:47.040
Is it, like, halfway to being a spy game?

00:07:47.480 --> 00:07:48.420
Uh, I meant stealth.

00:07:48.680 --> 00:07:51.580
Stealth. Okay. It's definitely a spy game because they'd say it is.

00:07:51.840 --> 00:07:54.300
Yes. Um, but I don't know.

00:07:54.360 --> 00:07:58.040
I think, like, I think this is, like, at least a prototype.

00:07:58.040 --> 00:07:59.840
It's a stealth game. Here's the weird thing, okay?

00:08:00.020 --> 00:08:02.760
If this is a spy game and it's 1983, we are...

00:08:02.760 --> 00:08:04.460
Stealth. Stealth or spy game?

00:08:04.540 --> 00:08:05.420
Yeah, stealth game, even.

00:08:05.640 --> 00:08:07.520
Stealth or spy game, and it's 1983.

00:08:08.520 --> 00:08:13.600
We are in, like, peak James Bond mode right now, where, like, Bond mania is high.

00:08:13.880 --> 00:08:15.680
Roger Moore is Bond.

00:08:15.680 --> 00:08:18.540
Like, you know, this is, like, all-time cheesiness levels.

00:08:18.840 --> 00:08:20.260
Things are getting really good.

00:08:20.420 --> 00:08:24.160
And then you get a game that's like, oh, yeah, yeah, yeah, what's the most spy thing you can do?

00:08:24.320 --> 00:08:26.560
Ride up and down the elevator and steal documents.

00:08:26.720 --> 00:08:33.160
It's like, where is, like, the remote control car that I'm gonna use to, like, knock people down off their feet?

00:08:33.240 --> 00:08:38.740
And where's, like, my explosive gel that I put on the door and then blow it up when they walk by?

00:08:38.740 --> 00:08:41.620
Like, that's what I'm looking for in my spy game.

00:08:41.740 --> 00:08:42.700
I like to imagine.

00:08:42.940 --> 00:08:51.880
Yeah, gadgets. I mean, I do think I sound silly just now talking about the first stealth game.

00:08:52.000 --> 00:08:53.040
No, I actually enjoy it.

00:08:53.160 --> 00:08:58.520
Now I also have, like, this image in my head of the pitch meeting.

00:08:58.520 --> 00:09:11.080
And it's similar to, like, how James Cameron pitched Aliens where he just wrote Aliens and then, like, made the S a dollar sign on a whiteboard.

00:09:11.460 --> 00:09:19.080
And so I like to think that they wrote Elevators or Elevator on a whiteboard.

00:09:19.520 --> 00:09:23.880
And then they just, like, took off a sheet and it said Action after it.

00:09:23.880 --> 00:09:26.480
Just, like, why were they thinking Elevator?

00:09:26.640 --> 00:09:30.680
Like, why is that the core gameplay loop?

00:09:30.880 --> 00:09:34.940
Yeah, yeah. I mean, the only thing I can, I mean, it doesn't answer that question at all.

00:09:35.200 --> 00:09:39.260
But the only thing I can think of is that, like, a lot of arcade games, there's, like, Burger Time.

00:09:39.340 --> 00:09:40.820
And there's, like, all these things that I'm just like, why is this a game?

00:09:40.820 --> 00:09:42.040
Which is also Tyto made.

00:09:42.220 --> 00:09:43.900
Is it? Yeah, Tyto did Burger Time.

00:09:44.040 --> 00:09:47.360
They were just picking up on all the big trends of the 80s, like Elevators.

00:09:47.360 --> 00:09:48.660
Like Elevators. And fast food.

00:09:49.140 --> 00:09:52.020
Yeah. I mean, like, if you think about it, though, we're kind of joking.

00:09:52.200 --> 00:09:57.700
But, like, the 80s would see, like, a lot of high-rise construction buildings in, like, in big cities.

00:09:57.700 --> 00:10:02.780
So this is actually a comment on the socioeconomic status of New York City in the 80s.

00:10:02.820 --> 00:10:04.040
I don't know. Absolutely.

00:10:04.720 --> 00:10:05.960
That's what I got from it.

00:10:06.980 --> 00:10:08.600
All right. So inputs.

00:10:08.860 --> 00:10:10.860
Are they responsive? Enough.

00:10:11.460 --> 00:10:13.580
I think enough. I think what you need.

00:10:13.740 --> 00:10:15.100
I mean, shooting's quick enough.

00:10:15.120 --> 00:10:16.780
Shooting is quick enough. And that's really, like, the biggest thing.

00:10:16.780 --> 00:10:19.040
Because it is you versus the other guy's bullet.

00:10:19.260 --> 00:10:20.460
So you want to be fast.

00:10:20.660 --> 00:10:27.640
Yeah. And I even, like, when I see the door open, I'll shoot early just to, like, anticipate how long it's going to take to travel over to them.

00:10:27.680 --> 00:10:29.940
But the bullets are a little faster than some of the other games.

00:10:30.040 --> 00:10:32.060
I always go back to, you know, Commando.

00:10:32.300 --> 00:10:34.460
Commando. Yeah, the bullet was so slow that you could.

00:10:34.460 --> 00:10:35.960
Those weren't really bullets. Those were, like, blue bubbles.

00:10:36.280 --> 00:10:40.200
Right. But these were, like, small and they were, like, fast enough that you couldn't.

00:10:40.660 --> 00:10:41.860
It was hard to dodge.

00:10:41.860 --> 00:10:42.680
Oh, you had to react. Yeah.

00:10:42.840 --> 00:10:50.680
Yeah, yeah, yeah. In fact, I think most of my deaths were met by, like, just not seeing a guy at the right time and then him shooting.

00:10:50.680 --> 00:10:55.180
And then, like, I'm trying to either duck or something and I wind up getting hit by his bullet.

00:10:55.180 --> 00:10:59.020
Yeah. You can die also by making direct contact with people, which is weird.

00:10:59.440 --> 00:11:01.320
Well, they grab you.

00:11:01.600 --> 00:11:03.140
Yeah, but why can't I grab them?

00:11:03.220 --> 00:11:05.920
Remember, this is an argument I'll always have with these games.

00:11:07.380 --> 00:11:18.020
I think that while, yeah, the controls themselves are fine, they're perfectly passable, I do think that the ways that you do interact with the environment can get a little fickle.

00:11:18.020 --> 00:11:26.020
Like, you have to stand, like, basically within two pixels of this white dot in order to open one of the red doors.

00:11:26.160 --> 00:11:27.860
And that's the same for the stairs, too.

00:11:27.860 --> 00:11:28.880
Yeah, yeah. It's just weird.

00:11:28.880 --> 00:11:32.000
And also, why am I taking the stairs in a game about elevators?

00:11:32.160 --> 00:11:34.420
Because that floor doesn't have stairs, Mike.

00:11:34.520 --> 00:11:35.500
It's an escalator.

00:11:36.400 --> 00:11:38.020
It's an escalator? Yeah, they are not.

00:11:39.520 --> 00:11:41.880
It's a completely mechanized moving system.

00:11:41.940 --> 00:11:44.100
I like that. The building itself is moving.

00:11:45.040 --> 00:11:45.860
Well, confirmed.

00:11:46.440 --> 00:11:47.720
Yeah, it's confirmed. Okay, great.

00:11:47.720 --> 00:11:50.700
Through space. Is there enough happening in this game, though?

00:11:50.860 --> 00:11:53.800
No. It needs something else.

00:11:53.920 --> 00:11:56.900
It needs another little piece.

00:11:57.140 --> 00:12:01.920
Right, because this is a game in 1987 that is living and dying by the high score mechanism.

00:12:02.720 --> 00:12:04.560
The whole thing is how high can you score?

00:12:04.640 --> 00:12:08.260
And it's like, that's not the experience I want to bring home into my living room.

00:12:08.260 --> 00:12:10.680
Right. Yeah, I think this would be okay in an arcade experience.

00:12:10.840 --> 00:12:13.840
You're just competing against whoever else trying to beat the score.

00:12:13.960 --> 00:12:15.500
Right, that local leaderboard at your bar.

00:12:16.180 --> 00:12:17.900
Right. Or Chuck E.

00:12:17.900 --> 00:12:19.260
Cheese. You're just like, I'm going to be at the top.

00:12:19.460 --> 00:12:20.380
Or Chuck E. Cheese. Yeah, or Chuck E. Cheese.

00:12:20.560 --> 00:12:21.480
Yeah, it depends on where you are.

00:12:21.960 --> 00:12:24.460
And then, just another question here.

00:12:24.920 --> 00:12:30.120
Is it weird that this simple game is being released on the NES now?

00:12:30.500 --> 00:12:37.860
1987. Would we have accepted it more in 1985 if this was a launch game?

00:12:37.860 --> 00:12:39.520
Would we have been kinder to it?

00:12:39.660 --> 00:12:41.760
Oh, very much so. Even us as in, the three of us?

00:12:41.760 --> 00:12:42.800
Yes, the three of us.

00:12:42.900 --> 00:12:47.520
I think I would have because I wasn't quite aware of what else I was getting into.

00:12:47.920 --> 00:12:52.800
Right. And here it's like, I've seen enough good games now, I don't want to see nothing like elevator

00:12:52.800 --> 00:12:59.080
action. I don't want to be too hard on it though, because I think at least it's something different.

00:12:59.480 --> 00:13:02.040
I mean, I know it's a remake, it's just a port.

